## BIP174 — "Partially Signed Bitcoin Transaction Format"

  https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0174.mediawiki
  Author: Ava Chow <me@achow101.com>
  Licensed under the 2-clause BSD license, per the BIP's own header
  (`License: BSD-2-Clause`) and its "Copyright" section ("This BIP is
  licensed under the 2-clause BSD license").

BIP174's "Test Vectors" section is itself part of the BIP text the above
license covers — its worked hex/Base64 examples are the BIP author's own
constructed data, not separately-licensed third-party material, but BSD-2-
Clause still requires this attribution be preserved when that data is
reproduced outside the BIP document itself, which is what `kat_vectors.zig`
does. Two groups of vectors are vendored, both hand/mechanically transcribed
from the section (never computed from this module's own output — see
`kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc comments for the exact provenance of each):

  * `invalid`/`valid`/`combiner_a`/`combiner_b`/`combiner_result` — the
    section's standalone Creator/Updater/Combiner example PSBTs, extracted
    programmatically (regex over the "Bytes in Hex" blocks) per
    `kat_vectors.zig`'s top-of-file doc comment. Drives `kat_test.zig`.

  * `finalize_combined_hex`/`finalize_finalized_hex`/
    `finalize_extracted_tx_hex` — the section's main multisig-workflow
    narrative's pre-finalize / post-finalize / post-extract triple (a bare
    P2SH 2-of-2 multisig input plus a P2SH-P2WSH 2-of-2 multisig input),
    hand-transcribed under the captions "a combiner must create this PSBT" /
    "an input finalizer must create this PSBT" / "a transaction extractor
    must create this Bitcoin transaction", cross-checked byte-for-byte
    against each vector's own Base64 sibling line before being committed.
    Drives `finalize_test.zig`'s "BIP174 official Finalizer/Extractor worked
    example" tests.

Implementation vs. test-data distinction: the PSBT wire-format CODEC and the
Input Finalizer/Transaction Extractor logic in `root.zig`/`finalize.zig` are
this module's own clean-room work from the public spec text — that needs no
attribution per root `NOTICE` §0 (a public spec is not a copyrightable work).
What is reproduced here, and only here, is the SPEC AUTHOR'S OWN WORKED-
EXAMPLE DATA, which is precisely why this notice exists and lives beside it
rather than in root `NOTICE` (per root `NOTICE` §1's policy: attribution for
reproduced third-party data travels with the module that owes it).

## Bitcoin Core `test/functional/data/rpc_psbt.json`

`src/core_kat_vectors.zig` reproduces conformance vectors from the Bitcoin
Core project's PSBT RPC functional-test data file (fetched 2026-08 from
`master`):

  Copyright (c) 2009-2010 Satoshi Nakamoto
  Copyright (c) 2009-present The Bitcoin Core developers
  Distributed under the MIT software license, see
  https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT

This adds no condition beyond MIT's own — the same "preserve the notice"
requirement already owed to BIP174's author above, owed here to a different
author. No third-party SOURCE CODE is present — only test-case DATA (base64
PSBTs, converted to hex to match this module's existing hex convention; see
`core_kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc comment for exactly which of the JSON's
`invalid`/`invalid_with_msg`/`valid`/`finalizer`/`extractor` arrays are
vendored, which entries are skipped as byte-identical BIP174 duplicates, and
what each vendored entry's disposition is (accepted as an intentional,
documented scope cut vs. rejected) — driven by `core_kat_test.zig`.

Two things worth flagging so a future reader doesn't have to re-derive them:
first, Core's own `finalizer`/`extractor` entries turned out to be
byte-identical to `kat_vectors.zig`'s BIP174 Finalizer/Extractor worked
example already vendored above (Core's functional test reuses BIP174's own
example verbatim rather than adding a new spend shape) — not re-vendored a
second time, to avoid duplicating an anchor. Second, working through Core's
`invalid`/`invalid_with_msg` sets surfaced one genuine BIP174 conformance gap
this module had (a present `PSBT_GLOBAL_VERSION` was never checked to
actually be `0`, per BIP174 §"Version 0") — now fixed in `root.zig`, with
Core's own vector as the external oracle. See `core_kat_test.zig`'s doc
comment for the full account, including which of Core's vectors are BIP370/
BIP371/MuSig2 content this module deliberately doesn't validate (not a bug).
